{
  "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/hate",
  "type": "Concept",
  "prefLabel": {
    "en": "hate"
  },
  "definition": {
    "en": "A negative emotional attitude characterised by a strong hostility towards others."
  },
  "scopeNote": {
    "en": [
      "Root causes of hate include fear of the unknown, perceived threats to one’s identity or resources, and deeply ingrained \"us-versus-them\" binary. Hate-related emotions combine disgust, contempt, and anger. In addition, hate is often regarded as the fundamental opposite of love, but some studies underline its spectrum, that is, individuals have the ability to change these emotions. Unlike fleeting anger, hate tends to be more enduring and deeply rooted. In psychology, self-hate can be the result of internalised fears and trauma. Hate is socially constructed and can trigger hate actions and even hate crimes."
    ]
  },
  "notation": [
    "13.7"
  ],
